The program can be used to discard portions of storage, and works
on either device files directly or upon files inside of a mounted
file‐system.   It  provides access to the system call, and relies
upon the backing device to have support in both the driver and in
the hardware itself.  The interface defaults to the mode and  re‐
quires the option to perform any actions.  Available options: Set
the  first  byte  to  be  discarded.   The default is 0.  Display
built‐in help.  Limit the length to be discarded.  The default is
the full device or file size, or the rest of the device  or  file
when the start offset has been set.  Set the per‐operation chunk‐
size.   The  default is 32 MiB.  Set the option, and do not actu‐
ally call secure erase, or write zeroes to the file.   This  flag
is  an  alias  for the flag.  This flag is an alias for the flag.
Turn off the Use secure erase operation.  This requires  specific
hardware  support  and  is  not currently supported.  Display the
program version and exit.  Set the verbose  flag.   Write  zeroes
instead  of  calling To have a dry‐run cleaning of any particular
file or device: # blkdiscard ‐n ‐v filename #  blkdiscard  ‐n  ‐v
/dev/rwd0b  To discard all the blocks on a swap device: # blkdis‐
card ‐v /dev/rwd0b To discard all but the first 256MiB of  a  de‐
vice,  using  128MiB  increments: # blkdiscard ‐v ‐f 256m ‐m 128m
/dev/rwd0c To zero out the  contents  of  a  file  or  device:  #
blkdiscard ‐v ‐z filename # blkdiscard ‐v ‐z /dev/device The com‐
